The base problem is that OpenSSL doesn't allow enough bits for errno on some platforms. This  doesn't seem to address the fact that, for example, errno is allowed to be an int.  I would expect an explicit errno field added:
```
struct err_state_st {
  int syserr[ERR_ NUM_ERRORS];
 ...
};
```
And then teach the "get error string" functions to look at syserr element for LIB_SYS errors.

Also, that cascading ifdef-based list is really ripe for auto-generating.

On 2020-07-01 14:48:49 [-0700], Richard Levitte wrote:
> > I had a patch where I shifted the upper bit so that it is gets not truncated. I don't mind restricting this shifting to Hurd and keep a test to find other platform with "different" number scheme where we would lose information.
> 
> That's *exactly* what this PR does

Okay.

> > I think this shifting didn't work because there is a list that saves the error numbers which are pre-generated at library start time.
> Why do we need that? According the the manpage for `strerror()' that string isn't modified by any other library function. This seems to be also true Hurd's libc.
> 
> We pre-load the error strings to fit into our error string structure.  Previous to this PR, we simply used the range 1..127, which doesn't fit what you'd expect on Hurd, since the POSIX numbers (which are the most commonly used) are in the range 0x40000001..0x40000079 (according to https://github.com/lattera/glibc/blob/master/sysdeps/mach/hurd/bits/errno.h), so for Hurd, we're entirely missing the target.

So, one more Hurd detail: strerror() for number which is not implemented
(defined) will segfault.

